I _usually_ cut original + quote marked articles
to the vim() editor, reflow the quoted parts to
my preferred text width (52 characters, to cater
to my astygmatic inability to read a longer line
easily) and then paste the text back into the
message area, as in the example above.

Those with what Hawk correctly calls "real news
readers" and which allow use of your visual editor
of choice make this easier, but I'm doing it to and
from a browser based posting tool where getting
quoted material narrower is much more important
because the (disgustingly incompetent) browser tool
text entry forms widgets _usually_ screw up quoted
text when it's too wide, wrapping it and then either
omitting the quoting on the wrapped part, or getting
its quoting level wrong, and do so with great glee.

Anyway, the glory part here is that vim() comes
out of the box expecting ">", ">>" and such on the
left to be quoting characters which it should
preserve while reflowing text, making it not only
easy to narrow quoted material, but to cut out
unneeded quoted material, reflow the rest
painlessly, and still leave the remnant looking
"pretty".

Emacs, from long ago memory, also lets you do this,
a little more painfully because the "quote prefix"
needs recapturing with each quoting level, IIRC, but
it still works. Emacs supports lots more _kinds_ of
quote prefixes than vim() does easily, though,
letting you choose to do fancier "stuff".

    [I used to parse complex multilogs with the
    speaker's ID as part of the prefix in each case,
    like "KPD>>> " as an example of quoting myself
    from three postings back in the thread, which
    made complex multiparty discussions easier for
    others to read; this was where emacs was clearly
    superior to vim(); doing that in vim() is
    (barely) doable, but at my knowledge level of
    vim(), it is purely an exercise in pain, where
    emacs makes the same task pretty easy.]

Bottom line is, if you use a good visual plain text
editor tool on your postings, _you_ can fix the
quoted line length, not worry that the OP hasn't
done that for you by using short lines in the
original posting.

Recommended, whether you're a vim() blind-worshipper
or an elisp-touting emacs bigot. <grin>

